e at  the  end of june but that doesn't change the  timings for these questions on  international travel  now having said all of that it is now  too early  to know where that the global travel  task force will come out  and know what the decision will be for  the 17th  of may and the reason for that is that  we are  seeing this third wave rising in some  parts of  europe and we're also seeing uh new  variants  and it is very important that we protect  the progress that we've been able to  make  uh here in the uk where we have we have  now in the prime minister yesterday i  mean he was talking about a third wave  you know  developing in europe and and washing up  on our shores when a wave hits our  european friends he said you know  it ends up in our shores it seems a  slightly strange way of talking about  it given that this third wave if you  want to call it that in europe emerged  you know  not far from where i'm standing in kent  we exported it  to the european union well it shows what  an international challenge this  this is and what we have to do  is all work as much as we can together  but it also shows why we're being  cautious on international travel because  know we've seen that that these waves  come  before we saw you know the first and  then the second  and they both ended up here  the the action that you took most  recently on our borders was  in your own words to prevent new  variants from arriving here to prevent  cases coming to the united kingdom why  should we be worried in any way  about a third wave happening on the  continent giving the changes that you've  made  well we should be concerned because we  have seen the impact before  and the the question that your system  we did absolutely but there's also we  also have a  um a desire if if possible if it's safe  to do so to open up  international travel of course people  want i understand people want a  summer holiday abroad if it's safe and  we've got to make that decision on on  whether it's sa